    {
      "id": "GENUS_SWIMA",
      "name": "Swima",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "Swima — a genus of polychaetas.",
      "lineage": "Swima",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "SP_SWIMA_BOMBIVIRIDIS_OSBORN_HADDOCK_PLEIJEL_MADIN_ROUSE_2009",
          "name": "Swima bombiviridis Osborn, Haddock, Pleijel, Madin & Rouse, 2009",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Swima",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "<p>External Description</p> <p>Body: Completely transparent body with 25 chaetigers with parapodial lobes and numerous long chaetae. The bottom end is smoothly tapered with a thick gelatinous sheath through which narrow, clavate papillae extend. Yellow, small papillae extend from the body wall through the sheath, especially numerous on parapodia.</p> <p>Head: The head has no eyes and consists of the prostomium, peristomium, and at least two achaetous segments possessing three forms of branchiae. Prostomium consists of tissue supporting a pair of low, ciliated, oblique ridges that form nuchal organs. Grooved frontal palps are transparent-yellow, tapered, and coiling at tips. Peristomium surrounds prostomium completely. Buccal organ is unarmed, bilobed, and form eversible lateral lips, which lack pigment.</p> <p>Branchiae: There are three forms of branchiae: 1) single, long (reach at least second chaetiger), tapered median branchia, transparent to white, 2) more than 40, fine, digitiform respiratory branchiae present across surface in tightly packed row, yellow, 3) four elliptical lobes attached to achaetous segments and one on first chaetiger.</p> <p>Bombs: The bombs are segmentally-occurring, elliptical, lobe-like branchiae, greenish-yellow in life, autofluoresce the same color, produce bioluminescence, and often autotomized. The \"bombs\" are 0.7 to 1.1 mm in length.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:2326138"
        },
        {
          "id": "SP_SWIMA_FULGIDA_OSBORN_HADDOCK_ROUSE_2011",
          "name": "Swima fulgida Osborn, Haddock & Rouse, 2011",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Swima",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "<p><em>Swima fulgida </em>is most similar to <em>S. bombiviridis</em>, so only distinguishing characterisitcs will be noted here. The presence of dark pigment on the<em> </em>foregut and lateral lips of the buccal organ, as well as<em> </em>the differences found in <em>COI </em>and cyt<em>b </em>sequences<em> </em>clearly separate the two species.<em> </em>The foregut is darkly pigmented, appearing black through body wall, and is expandable to near the body width. Additionally, specimens of <em>S. fulgida </em>tend to be larger than those of <em>S. bombiviridis </em>and are broadest after the third chaetiger, unlike <em>S. bombiviridis</em>, which tapers from the head and first chaetiger. <em>S. fulgida </em>also has fewer prominent interramal lollipop-shaped papillae. </p><p>[Osborn et al. 2011]</p>",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:5854408"
        },
        {
          "id": "SP_SWIMA_TAWITAWIENSIS_OSBORN_HADDOCK_ROUSE_2011",
          "name": "Swima tawitawiensis Osborn, Haddock & Rouse, 2011",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Swima",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "<p><em>Swima tawitawiensis </em>is most similar to <em>S. fulgida </em>and <em>S. bombiviridis, </em>so only distinguishing characterisitcs will be noted here. <em>S. tawitawiensis </em>possesses a pair of lateral subulate branchiae, which is absent in both other species. The medial subulate and the lateral projections are branchiae because of the large blood vessels that run their length and originate from the heart body. These structures possess no obvious sensillae. All three projections appear to originate from a single achaetous segment. The broad, flattened notochaetae and compound neurochaetae further differentiate <em>S. tawitawiensis </em>from the other <em>Swima </em>species, as do the less pronounced interramal lollipop-shaped papillae and U-shaped medial end of the nuchal organs.</p> <p><em>S. tawitawiensis </em>and <em>S. fulgida </em>share a darkly pigmented foregut. The posterior-most pair of elliptical branchiae scars of <em>S. tawitawiensis</em> is not on the first chaetiger as found in the other two <em>Swima </em>species, but instead they lie on an additional achaetous segment. The body-wall projections supporting the small ring-like scars where the elliptical branchiae were attached also differentiate <em>S.</em> <em>tawitawiensis </em>from the other two <em>Swima</em> species.</p><p>[Osborn et al. 2011]</p>",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:6453582"
        }
      ]
    },
